Behind the beaches, the west of Mauritius rises steeply into mountains and forest. It is where the island's best hikes are, from a short climb with a view of the lagoon to a full day in the national park.

## Le Morne Brabant

[Le Morne Brabant](/en/places/le-morne-brabant) rises 556 metres above the lagoon at the southwest tip of the island. A trail climbs from the eastern side: the lower part is an easy walk with views of the reef that widen as you climb, the upper part steep, with some scrambling. Access to the top has been regulated at times, so check the current rules before you go; a licensed local guide can also tell you the mountain's history as a refuge for people escaping slavery.

## Black River Gorges National Park

[Black River Gorges](/en/places/black-river-gorges-national-park) is the largest national park in Mauritius, about 67 km² of native forest, ravines and heathland with some 60 km of trails. Walks start both near the coast at Black River and from the plateau around Plaine Champagne. Even if you do not hike, the [Gorges Viewpoint](/en/places/gorges-viewpoint) and [Alexandra Falls](/en/places/alexandra-falls) give a sense of the landscape from the roadside.

Look out for the park's endemic birds, saved from the edge of extinction: the Mauritius kestrel, the pink pigeon and the echo parakeet.

## Piton de la Petite Rivière Noire

At 828 metres, [Piton de la Petite Rivière Noire](/en/places/piton-de-la-petite-riviere-noire) is the highest summit in Mauritius. The most common route starts from Plaine Champagne; others begin near Chamarel and at the national park's visitor centre. The last section is steep and exposed.

## When to go

- The cooler, drier months from May to October are the most comfortable for walking.
- Start early, whatever the season: it is cooler, the views are clearer, and clouds often build over the hills by midday.
- Avoid steep trails after heavy rain, when paths become slippery.

## Safety

- Take plenty of water, sun protection and a light rain layer.
- Wear proper shoes, not beach sandals.
- Tell someone your route, keep to marked paths, and turn back if the weather changes.
- On unfamiliar or exposed routes, go with a licensed local guide.
